WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.889
Welcome to your first R lesson.

2
00:00:02.889 --> 00:00:05.673
Take a deep breath, there is no rush.

3
00:00:05.673 --> 00:00:10.547
In this lesson, you will meet RStudio, write your very first lines of code,

4
00:00:10.547 --> 00:00:12.983
and see R give you the answers.

5
00:00:12.983 --> 00:00:15.420
We will go step by step, together.

6
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:18.368
This is RStudio.

7
00:00:18.368 --> 00:00:22.640
On the top left is the script, where we write our code.

8
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:26.200
Below it is the console, where R shows the results.

9
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.760
On the right, you see the environment, and your files.

10
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.619
Let's start with something simple.

11
00:00:33.619 --> 00:00:37.984
I write a small note first, with a hash sign: Basic Calculation.

12
00:00:37.984 --> 00:00:40.531
Anything after a hash is a comment.

13
00:00:40.531 --> 00:00:42.350
R does not run it.

14
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:45.260
It is only there to help us remember.

15
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.196
Now our first line: two plus five.

16
00:00:51.196 --> 00:00:54.962
Put your cursor on the line, and press Control and Enter.

17
00:00:54.962 --> 00:00:57.700
On a Mac, it is Command and Enter.

18
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.276
Look at the console. R says seven.

19
00:01:02.276 --> 00:01:05.460
Wonderful, you just ran your first piece of code!

20
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:07.562
Next line:

21
00:01:07.562 --> 00:01:09.848
five hundred seventy four minus four.

22
00:01:09.848 --> 00:01:11.754
Run it the same way.

23
00:01:11.754 --> 00:01:14.040
The answer is five hundred seventy.

24
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:16.490
Now a longer one:

25
00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:21.561
two plus five divided by three, plus ten times eight, minus nine.

26
00:01:21.561 --> 00:01:24.096
R follows the usual math order.

27
00:01:24.096 --> 00:01:27.899
It divides and multiplies first, then adds and subtracts.

28
00:01:27.899 --> 00:01:31.280
The answer is seventy four point six six.

29
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:46.304
Notice how every result in the console starts with a one in brackets.

30
00:01:46.304 --> 00:01:50.114
That just means this is the first value of the answer.

31
00:01:50.114 --> 00:01:51.500
Nothing to worry about.

32
00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.277
Next, I add a new heading: R Data Types.

33
00:01:55.277 --> 00:01:57.152
Under it, three more notes:

34
00:01:57.152 --> 00:01:58.653
character, numeric, and logical.

35
00:01:58.653 --> 00:02:02.780
These are the three kinds of values we will meet first.

36
00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.287
Let's begin with the character type.

37
00:02:29.287 --> 00:02:32.604
A character is simply text, like a name.

38
00:02:32.604 --> 00:02:35.920
Text in R always goes inside quotation marks.

39
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.638
Here I type a name inside quotes, as our first example.

40
00:02:43.638 --> 00:02:47.824
In the next lesson, we will look at each data type closely.

41
00:02:47.824 --> 00:02:49.220
You did great today!

